statement:
Newton's cradle is a famous experiment in which a row of steel balls (let's assume five of them) are suspended by strings. When the leftmost ball is pulled to the left and released, it falls down under gravity's pull and collides with the four remaining stationary balls. The result is that four balls (the three middle ones, plus the one that just fell) are stationary and the one rightmost ball shoots out of the right hand side of the cradle.

Estimate the time delay between when the leftmost ball strikes the four other balls and when the rightmost ball ceases to have contact with the other balls.

You may assume the following: \begin{aligned} \text{ball diameter } (d) & = 2 \;\mathrm{cm} \\ \text{Young's modulus of steel } (Y) & = 200 \;\mathrm{GPa} \\ \text{density of steel } (\rho) & = 7.8 \cdot 10^3 \;\mathrm{kg \cdot m^{-3}} \end{aligned}

An artificial satellite emitting a constant frequency radio signal passes by an observing station which records the variation of received frequency at $$20 \;\mathrm{s}$$ intervals as follows:

\begin{aligned} 40.00215 &\;\mathrm{MHz}\\ 40.00208 &\;\mathrm{MHz}\\ 40.00196 &\;\mathrm{MHz}\\ 40.00175 &\;\mathrm{MHz}\\ 40.00141 &\;\mathrm{MHz}\\ 40.00106 &\;\mathrm{MHz}\\ 40.00077 &\;\mathrm{MHz}\\ 40.00059 &\;\mathrm{MHz}\\ 40.00049 &\;\mathrm{MHz}\\ 40.00043 &\;\mathrm{MHz} \end{aligned}

Assuming a straight trajectory for the satellite, calculate its speed and closest distance of approach.
